

1. Executive Summary & Incident Scope

The Retail Core Banking & Order Processing API is experiencing severe performance degradation on database-intensive REST endpoints.

Analysis using Google’s Four Golden Signals and Brendan Gregg’s USE methodology identifies the Storage / Database Connection Pool as the primary infrastructure bottleneck.

The database connection pool is operating at 100% utilization. The infrastructure telemetry shows 210 threads waiting for database connections and 2,190 related errors.

The two critically degraded endpoints are:

POST /api/v1/checkout
POST /api/v1/reports/export

POST /api/v1/checkout has:

Traffic: 340 RPS
P50 latency: 280 ms
P99 latency: 6800 ms
Average error latency: 5200 ms
Error rate: 17.65%
HTTP 5xx count: 1800
Worker-thread utilization: 98%
Database connections: 50/50
DB waiting requests: 142

POST /api/v1/reports/export has:

Traffic: 45 RPS
P50 latency: 1200 ms
P99 latency: 11500 ms
Average error latency: 10200 ms
Error rate: 28.89%
HTTP 5xx count: 390
Database connections: 50/50
DB waiting requests: 68

In comparison, GET /api/v1/items/{id} remains healthy even at 580 RPS. This demonstrates that traffic volume itself is not the root cause.

CPU utilization is only 41.5%, while memory utilization is 62.4%. Both remain below their warning thresholds. Therefore, CPU and memory are not the primary bottlenecks.

Primary diagnosis: Database connection pool exhaustion is causing request queuing, thread saturation, increased P99 latency, and HTTP 5xx failures.

