Latency

Latency measures the amount of time required to complete a request.

P50 represents a typical request, while P99 shows the experience of the slowest requests.

The degraded endpoints show very high tail latency:

Checkout P99: 6800 ms
Reports/export P99: 11500 ms

Healthy endpoints show much lower P99 latency:

Items: 95 ms
Search: 280 ms
Health: 12 ms

Error latency is important because failed requests may spend several seconds waiting for a constrained database connection before eventually returning an error.

Traffic

Traffic represents the amount of demand placed on the application.

GET /api/v1/items/{id} handles 580 RPS and remains healthy.

GET /api/v1/search handles 420 RPS and also remains healthy.

POST /api/v1/checkout handles only 340 RPS but is critically degraded.

POST /api/v1/reports/export processes only 45 RPS but is also critically degraded.

Therefore, high traffic alone is not responsible for the incident. Database dependency and resource consumption are more important than raw RPS.

Errors

The error analysis shows:

Items: 0 HTTP 5xx, 0.0% error rate
Checkout: 1800 HTTP 5xx, 17.65% error rate
Search: 0 HTTP 5xx, 0.0% error rate
Reports/export: 390 HTTP 5xx, 28.89% error rate
Health: 0 HTTP 5xx, 0.0% error rate

HTTP 4xx errors generally represent client-side request problems.

HTTP 5xx errors indicate server-side or downstream dependency failures.

The large number of HTTP 5xx responses from checkout and reports/export therefore indicates a backend resource problem.

Saturation

Saturation indicates how close a resource is to its maximum effective capacity.

The strongest saturation evidence is the database connection pool.

POST /api/v1/checkout:

DB connections: 50/50
DB waiting requests: 142
Worker-thread utilization: 98%

POST /api/v1/reports/export:

DB connections: 50/50
DB waiting requests: 68

By comparison, GET /api/v1/items/{id} uses only 8/50 database connections and 12% worker threads.

This demonstrates that database connection saturation is strongly correlated with the degraded endpoints.

